# Normalise line endings: commit LF, check out LF.
#
# Without this, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF as CRLF
# on the way out. That is harmless for Gradle but not for web/, which is a
# committed build artifact of the web app: rewriting the JS and CSS there
# produces a dirty tree immediately after a fresh clone, and the diff is
# unreviewable.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The JVM and the Android toolchain are happiest with LF, and gradlew must not
# acquire a line-ending problem.
android/** text eol=lf
gradlew text eol=lf
*.gradle text eol=lf
*.java text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.kt text eol=lf
*.pro text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf

# Binaries, LAST, because in .gitattributes the last matching rule wins. Every
# rule above is a prefix match that would otherwise claim the files below. A
# text conversion on a poster JPEG or a PNG is silent corruption that only
# surfaces much later as a broken image, and a corrupt .jar fails the build with
# an unrelated-looking error.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.jar binary
*.aar binary
*.so binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary

# The Gradle wrapper jar is committed on purpose: it is how a fresh clone gets
# a known-good Gradle, and gradle-wrapper.properties carries the SHA256 it gets
# checked against. Binary so nothing rewrites it.
gradle-wrapper.jar binary
